import Foundation

// MARK: - Prayer Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let prayerContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [

        // MARK: Entry 9 — Morning Prayer
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 5:3",
            scriptureText: "In the morning, LORD, you hear my voice; in the morning I lay my requests before you and wait expectantly.",
            devotionalTitle: "First Words of the Day",
            devotionalText: "David didn't begin his mornings by scrolling or rushing. He began by laying his requests before God and then waiting expectantly — not anxiously, but with the confidence of a child who knows his Father will answer. There is something uniquely powerful about giving God the first moments of your day. Before the world sets the agenda, you can let the Creator set yours. Morning prayer isn't about earning God's favor; it's about orienting your heart toward the One who already holds your day in His hands. What if tomorrow morning, your first words belonged to Him?",
            prayerText: "Father, I give You the first moments of this day. Before I check my phone, before I make my plans, I lay my requests before You. You already know what today holds, and I trust that You are good. Give me ears to hear Your voice above the noise and a heart that waits expectantly for Your answer. Teach me the discipline of meeting You in the morning so that I walk through every hour grounded in Your presence. I choose to begin with You. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What currently has the 'first word' in your mornings, and how might giving that place to God change the rest of your day?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Tomorrow morning, pray before looking at your phone — even if it's just one sentence of surrender",
                "Ask a friend or family member to join you in a morning prayer challenge for one week"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 10 — Ask, Seek, Knock
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Luke 11:9-10",
            scriptureText: "So I say to you: Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and the door will be opened to you. For everyone who asks receives; the one who seeks finds; and to the one who knocks, the door will be opened.",
            devotionalTitle: "The God Who Opens Doors",
            devotionalText: "Jesus uses three escalating images here: asking, seeking, and knocking. Each one requires a little more effort, a little more persistence, a little more intentionality. This isn't a vending machine promise — it's an invitation into a relationship with a Father who loves to give good gifts. God is not reluctant or withholding; He is generous and eager to respond. Sometimes the answer comes quickly; sometimes seeking and knocking are the very process through which God shapes our hearts. The point is this: never stop coming to Him.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, You invite me to ask boldly, seek earnestly, and knock persistently. Forgive me for the times I've given up too quickly or assumed You weren't listening. I come to You now with open hands and an expectant heart. Teach me the difference between demanding my own way and trusting Your timing. I ask for faith that endures even when the door hasn't opened yet. Thank You that You are a generous Father who delights in answering Your children. I will keep knocking. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a prayer you have stopped praying because you assumed God's silence meant 'no'?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write down one 'big ask' prayer and commit to praying it daily for the next 30 days",
                "Encourage someone who is waiting on God by sharing a time He answered your prayer unexpectedly"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 11 — Jesus' Solitary Prayer
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Mark 1:35",
            scriptureText: "Very early in the morning, while it was still dark, Jesus got up, left the house and went off to a solitary place, where he prayed.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Rhythm Jesus Chose",
            devotionalText: "If anyone could have skipped prayer, it was Jesus. He is God in the flesh. Yet even He withdrew to solitary places to pray. That tells us something profound: prayer is not a sign of weakness but of wisdom. Jesus modeled a rhythm of withdrawal and engagement — solitude with the Father fueling service to the world. The demands on His time were enormous, yet He prioritized communion with the Father above every crowd and every need. If the Son of God needed this rhythm, how much more do we?",
            prayerText: "Jesus, You showed me what it looks like to prioritize the Father above every demand and distraction. I confess that I often let busyness crowd out my time with God. Give me the courage to withdraw — to say no to noise so I can say yes to You. Create in me a hunger for solitude that isn't escapism but is fuel for the life You've called me to live. Help me to find my solitary place, even in a crowded life, and meet me there with Your presence. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What would you need to say 'no' to in order to create a regular solitary place for prayer?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Identify one 15-minute window this week to pray alone in a quiet place with no distractions",
                "Share with someone you trust that you are building a prayer rhythm and ask them to hold you accountable"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 12 — Praying in the Spirit
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ephesians 6:18",
            scriptureText: "And pray in the Spirit on all occasions with all kinds of prayers and requests. With this in mind, be alert and always keep on praying for all the Lord's people.",
            devotionalTitle: "Prayer as Spiritual Armor",
            devotionalText: "Paul places this command at the climax of his famous 'armor of God' passage. Prayer isn't just one piece of the armor — it is the atmosphere in which every other piece functions. Without prayer, faith becomes self-reliance, and truth becomes mere information. Notice the repeated word 'all': all occasions, all kinds of prayers, all the Lord's people. Prayer is meant to be woven into the fabric of every moment, every relationship, and every battle. When you pray, you are not retreating from the fight — you are engaging in it at the deepest level.",
            prayerText: "Heavenly Father, I put on the full armor You have given me and I take my stand in prayer. Teach me to pray on all occasions — in joy and in sorrow, in victory and in struggle. Keep me alert to the needs around me and faithful in interceding for Your people. I pray not in my own strength but in the power of Your Spirit. Guard my heart against discouragement and my mind against distraction. Let my prayer life be the foundation of every battle I face today. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How would your daily struggles change if you saw prayer as your primary weapon rather than your last resort?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Before facing a difficult situation today, pause and pray silently for 60 seconds, asking the Spirit to lead you",
                "Choose one person in your church or community and commit to praying for them by name every day this week"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 13 — Devoted to Prayer
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Colossians 4:2",
            scriptureText: "Devote yourselves to prayer, being watchful and thankful.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Discipline of Devotion",
            devotionalText: "Paul's instruction is deceptively simple: devote yourselves to prayer. The word 'devote' carries the idea of persistent, steady commitment — the kind of faithfulness that doesn't depend on feelings. He adds two postures: watchfulness and thankfulness. Watchfulness keeps our prayers grounded in reality — aware of what God is doing and what the enemy is attempting. Thankfulness keeps our prayers grounded in faith — remembering that God has already been faithful. Together, they transform prayer from a duty into a deeply rooted way of life.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I want to be devoted to prayer — not in bursts of passion that fade, but with steady, rooted faithfulness. Open my eyes to be watchful: show me where You are moving and where I need to stand firm. Fill my mouth with thankfulness, even in seasons that are hard to understand. Forgive me for treating prayer as optional rather than essential. Build in me a devotion that lasts, sustained not by willpower but by Your Spirit. Let my prayer life reflect the steadiness of Your love. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What is the difference between praying when you feel like it and being devoted to prayer?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Start a simple prayer journal — write one prayer and one thanksgiving each day for a week",
                "Text or call one person today and ask, 'How can I pray for you this week?' — then follow through"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 14 — The Nearness of God
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 145:18",
            scriptureText: "The LORD is near to all who call on him, to all who call on him in truth.",
            devotionalTitle: "Closer Than You Think",
            devotionalText: "Sometimes prayer feels like sending a message into space — you wonder if anyone is on the other end. But the psalmist declares that God is near to all who call on Him. Not distant. Not distracted. Near. The one condition is truth: calling on Him sincerely, not performing or pretending. God is not impressed by polished prayers; He is moved by honest ones. You don't have to clean yourself up to approach Him. Come as you are — messy, uncertain, broken — and discover that He is already closer than your next breath.",
            prayerText: "Father, thank You that You are near — not far off, not uninterested, but close to me right now. I call on You in truth today. I bring You my real self: my doubts, my fears, my gratitude, my longing. I don't want to perform for You; I want to meet You. Help me to sense Your nearness in the ordinary moments of my day. When I feel alone, remind me that You are as close as a whispered prayer. I rest in the truth that You draw near to those who draw near to You. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "When was the last time you were completely honest with God in prayer — no editing, no performing?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Pray one fully honest prayer today — tell God exactly how you feel without filtering your words",
                "Reach out to someone who seems lonely or discouraged and remind them that God is near"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 15 — Before You Call
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 65:24",
            scriptureText: "Before they call I will answer; while they are still speaking I will hear.",
            devotionalTitle: "The God Who Anticipates",
            devotionalText: "This verse reveals something staggering about God's character: He is not reactive but proactive. Before the prayer leaves your lips, He is already moving. While you are still forming the words, He is already listening. This doesn't make prayer unnecessary — it makes prayer an act of joining what God is already doing. Prayer aligns your heart with a God who is perpetually ahead of you, preparing answers before you even know the questions. You are never catching God off guard; you are catching up to His grace.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I am amazed that You answer before I even call. You are already at work in situations I haven't begun to pray about. Forgive me for thinking I need to convince You to care — You cared first. Help me to see prayer not as informing You but as aligning myself with what You are already doing. Give me the faith to trust Your timing, even when I can't see Your hand moving. Thank You that Your grace is always ahead of me. I rest in Your perfect knowledge of my needs. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How does knowing God is already at work — before you even pray — change the way you approach Him?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Look back over the past month and write down one prayer God answered in a way you didn't expect",
                "Share that story of answered prayer with someone who needs encouragement today"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 16 — Ask and Receive
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 7:7-8",
            scriptureText: "Ask and it will be given to you; seek and you will find; knock and the door will be opened to you. For everyone who asks receives; the one who seeks finds; and to the one who knocks, the door will be opened.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Generosity of the Father",
            devotionalText: "Jesus repeats this promise in the Sermon on the Mount to emphasize a truth we constantly forget: God is generous. He is not a reluctant giver who must be begged into kindness. He is a Father who loves to respond to His children. The progression from asking to seeking to knocking suggests that prayer is not passive — it is an active, persistent, faith-filled pursuit of God. And the promise is absolute: everyone who asks receives. The answer may not look like what we expected, but the Father's response is always rooted in love and wisdom.",
            prayerText: "Generous Father, thank You that You invite me to ask boldly. I confess that I sometimes pray small prayers because I have a small view of Your goodness. Expand my faith today. Help me to ask with confidence, seek with persistence, and knock with expectation. I trust that Your answer is always better than my request. Teach me to hold my desires with open hands, knowing that You give not what I demand but what I truly need. I receive Your generosity with a grateful heart. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Are you praying small prayers because you secretly doubt God's willingness to give generously?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write down three things you have been hesitant to ask God for, and bring each one to Him in prayer today",
                "Demonstrate God's generosity to someone else — surprise a friend, coworker, or stranger with an unexpected act of kindness"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 17 — Abiding and Asking
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "John 15:7",
            scriptureText: "If you remain in me and my words remain in you, ask whatever you wish, and it will be done for you.",
            devotionalTitle: "Rooted Requests",
            devotionalText: "This promise sounds almost too good to be true: ask whatever you wish. But Jesus anchors it in a vital condition — remaining in Him. When we abide in Christ and His words abide in us, our desires begin to change. We stop asking for what merely pleases us and start asking for what pleases Him. Abiding is not a technique; it is a relationship of deep, daily dependence. The more deeply rooted we are in Jesus, the more our prayers align with His heart — and the more powerfully He answers them.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, I want to remain in You — not just visit You occasionally, but make my home in Your presence. Let Your words sink deep into my heart and reshape my desires from the inside out. I confess the times I've treated prayer like a wish list rather than a conversation with my Savior. Align my asking with Your will. As I abide in You, make my prayers bolder and my trust deeper. I want to want what You want. Bear fruit through my life that only comes from being rooted in You. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How might your prayer requests change if you spent more time abiding in Christ before asking?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Before your next prayer time, spend five minutes reading Scripture and sitting quietly with Jesus before making any requests",
                "Ask someone close to you what they see as the 'fruit' in your life and where they see room for growth"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 18 — Confidence in Prayer
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 John 5:14-15",
            scriptureText: "This is the confidence we have in approaching God: that if we ask anything according to his will, he hears us. And if we know that he hears us — whatever we ask — we know that we have what we asked of him.",
            devotionalTitle: "Praying With Confidence",
            devotionalText: "John gives us a remarkable basis for confidence in prayer: God hears us. Not sometimes. Not when we perform well enough. He hears us when we pray according to His will. This transforms prayer from a guessing game into a partnership. We don't have to wonder whether our prayers reach heaven — they do. The key is learning to align our requests with God's revealed will, which we find in His Word. As we grow in knowing Scripture, we grow in praying confidently, because we begin to pray the very things God longs to do.",
            prayerText: "Father, I come to You with confidence — not in myself, but in Your promise that You hear me. Teach me Your will through Your Word so that my prayers align with Your purposes. I don't want to pray timidly when You have invited me to come boldly. Remove the doubt that whispers my prayers don't matter. I believe that You hear me right now, and I trust that You are faithful to answer according to Your perfect wisdom. Let my confidence grow as my knowledge of Your will deepens. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What would it feel like to pray with genuine confidence that God hears every word?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Choose one promise from Scripture and pray it back to God, claiming it with confidence",
                "Encourage a fellow believer who is struggling in prayer by sharing 1 John 5:14-15 with them"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 19 — Approaching the Throne
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Hebrews 4:16",
            scriptureText: "Let us then approach God's throne of grace with confidence, so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time of need.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Throne of Grace",
            devotionalText: "In the ancient world, approaching a king's throne uninvited could cost you your life. But the writer of Hebrews flips that image entirely. Because of Jesus, the throne is no longer a throne of judgment for believers — it is a throne of grace. We are not only permitted to approach; we are urged to come with confidence. And what do we find there? Mercy for our failures and grace precisely calibrated to our moment of need. Prayer is the act of walking into the throne room of the universe and finding not a distant monarch but a loving Father with arms wide open.",
            prayerText: "Gracious God, I approach Your throne boldly — not because of anything I've done, but because of everything Jesus has done for me. Thank You that Your throne is one of grace, not condemnation. I need Your mercy today for the places where I've fallen short. I need Your grace for the challenges that feel bigger than me. Meet me in my moment of need, Father. Help me to come to You freely and frequently, never forgetting the access that the cross has purchased for me. I receive Your mercy now. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you tend to approach God as a distant judge or as a gracious Father — and what shapes that view?",
            actionSteps: [
                "When you pray today, begin by thanking Jesus for giving you access to the Father's throne",
                "If you know someone burdened by guilt or shame, gently share this verse and remind them that grace — not judgment — awaits them"
            ]
        ),

        // MARK: Entry 20 — The Cry of the Righteous
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 34:17",
            scriptureText: "The righteous cry out, and the LORD hears them; he delivers them from all their troubles.",
            devotionalTitle: "When You Cry Out",
            devotionalText: "David wrote this psalm from a place of real desperation — he had been on the run, pretending to be insane to save his own life. And yet, from that low place, he testified that God hears the cry of the righteous. Notice that it doesn't say God prevents all trouble. It says He delivers through it. Prayer in times of trouble isn't wishful thinking; it's a lifeline to the God who rescues. Your cry doesn't have to be articulate or composed. It just has to be real. God responds not to perfection but to the honest, desperate cry of a heart that trusts Him.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I cry out to You today. You know the troubles I'm facing — some I can name, some I can barely understand. But I trust that You hear me. You have delivered me before, and I believe You will deliver me again. Give me the courage to be honest about my struggles instead of hiding behind pretended strength. I don't need to have it all together to come to You. Meet me in my trouble, Father. Be my rescuer, my refuge, and my hope. I trust in Your deliverance. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What trouble are you facing right now that you need to honestly cry out to God about?",
            actionSteps: [
                "Write down the trouble weighing on you most and pray Psalm 34:17 over it specifically",
                "Check in with someone in your life who may be going through a hard season and offer to pray with them"
            ]
        )
    ]
}
